**One pipeline problem, fixed:** the voice service started rejecting the voice name `onyx` partway through today (404 on every beat; the three-million-invoices build had worked with it minutes earlier). I compared the service's voices against an earlier `onyx` recording of the same line: `onyx` had been playing as the voice Atlas (same pitch, same length, closest sound). I added one line to `shorts/build_short.py` that swaps `onyx` for Atlas, so every existing script keeps its usual voice. This also fixes the other film builds running right now, which would otherwise hit the same error.

**Voiceover:**
1. *"We sincerely apologize for our actions. I would not want to harm your business, but we have no other choice. An email to Bludorn, a Houston restaurant. July, twenty twenty-two."* — on screen: "WE SINCERELY APOLOGIZE"
2. *"The week after the Fourth of July, one-star ratings had appeared on its Google page. No words attached. In one week, four point eight fell to four point five."* — "4.8 TO 4.5"
3. *"The email said there would be one review a day. To stop it: a seventy-five dollar Google Play gift card. Resold, the note said, the card meant three weeks of income for the sender's family."* — "$75 GIFT CARD"
4. *"Google at first said the ratings broke no policy, because there were no comments. The owners didn't pay. They posted the email. About a hundred customers answered with reviews."* — "THEY DIDN'T PAY"
5. *"When Google finally removed the fake ratings, most of the real five-star reviews went too. The rating settled at four point six."* — "4.6"
6. *"The apology was a form letter. The New York Times reported the text was the same in each email, sent to restaurants from San Francisco to New York. One-star ratings with no words. And a gift card, because, one consultant said, such transactions are difficult to trace."* — "THE SAME IN EACH EMAIL"

**`claims.json`** (`bizcrime/stories/apologetic-ransom/claims.json`): each of the 19 voiceover sentences maps to its source URL and the quoted line it rests on. A script checked that all 32 quotes appear word for word in the saved sources (0 missing), and that a made-up quote correctly fails the same check. Joined together, the claims sentences match the voiceover exactly. The ransom note is sourced to TODAY, not CBS, as the verified brief requires.

**What I softened or left out because the sources don't support it:**
- **No review count.** TODAY says "a few," the Houston Chronicle says 30 or more.
- **"About a hundred" customers,** where the two sources say "more than 100" and "around 100."
- **"Sorry" never appears.** That word was in other restaurants' notes. Bludorn's note says "We sincerely apologize."
- **The scammer's claims are only "the note said."** That covers "three weeks of income"; the India claim is left out entirely.
- **"July 2022" only,** because no source dates the email itself.
- **No named aliases.** The pseudonym on the note isn't used.
- **No "untraceable"** as a flat fact. The film uses the consultant's "difficult to trace," credited to him.
- **Nothing about arrests or the FBI being involved.** The film ends on how the scheme worked, not on a resolution.
- **Left out:** the Chicago escalation email (it went to a different restaurant) and any claim that Bludorn lost business (CBS says it didn't).
- **Caption:** it says "don't pay, report to the FTC and the FBI's IC3" is the National Restaurant Association's advice. Only the "don't pay" part is also attributed to Google in the source.
- **The caption's source line names two outlets:** "Source: TODAY.com, July 29, 2022; CBS News, August 3, 2022." There is no court or agency record for this story, and the rating numbers and the note come from TODAY while the owners' refusal and Google's first response come from CBS.

